		'option_d'       => 'Remove the other partner’s name and send the remainder automatically without further assessment of the governing facts.',
		'correct_option' => 'c',
		'explanation'    => 'A. Incorrect. One client may not have authority to authorize disclosure of every other person’s information contained in a joint record.
B. Incorrect. Couple records are not categorically exempt from release. Disclosure may be possible after appropriate analysis and authorization.
C. Correct. A multi-person record may contain protected information about more than one client or participant. One partner’s signature does not automatically resolve the other partner’s confidentiality interests. The therapist should evaluate: Who holds client status How the record was structured What policies governed separate communications Whether the authorization covers the proposed disclosure Whether the signer may authorize release of the entire record Whether information can be separated or disclosed more narrowly
D. Incorrect. Removing a name may not adequately protect the other partner. The remaining information may still identify that person or reveal confidential communications.
