	array(
		'question_text'  => 'A therapist begins responding immediately to a client’s nonurgent text messages late every night. Over time, the client increasingly asks the therapist to make routine personal decisions.What is the most appropriate response?',
		'option_a'       => 'Continue responding because immediate availability demonstrates commitment without additional assessment or consultation.',
		'option_b'       => 'Assess the pattern, discuss its effect on dependency, clarify communication expectations, and restore appropriate limits.',
		'option_c'       => 'Continue until the client directly complains about becoming dependent without additional assessment or consultation.',
		'option_d'       => 'Stop responding to every message without discussing the change with the client before considering a less disruptive protective response.',
		'correct_option' => 'b',
		'explanation'    => 'A. Incorrect. Frequent access may feel caring, but good intentions do not eliminate the risk of dependency or role confusion. The effect on the client is more important than the therapist’s belief that the behavior demonstrates commitment.
B. Correct. The therapist should evaluate how the pattern of late-night availability is affecting role clarity, autonomy, and treatment. The client’s increasing reliance on the therapist for ordinary decisions suggests that the communication pattern may be encouraging dependency. A client-centered response would include discussing what has developed, clarifying when and how messages will be handled, identifying appropriate crisis procedures, and monitoring whether the client becomes more able to function independently.
