		'option_c'       => 'Require the minor to prove that retaliation will occur beyond all doubt without further assessment of the governing facts.',
		'option_d'       => 'Consult with the minor, assess whether parent involvement would be inappropriate, and document the reason for the professional determination.',
		'correct_option' => 'd',
		'explanation'    => 'A. Incorrect. Immediate contact could expose the minor to the very harm being evaluated. The statute requires consultation with the minor before the therapist makes the determination.
B. Incorrect. The therapist should not make an unlimited promise before assessing the circumstances, applicable confidentiality limits, and future developments.
C. Incorrect. The statute calls for a professional determination. It does not require the minor to prove future harm beyond all doubt.
D. Correct. The therapist must consult with the minor before determining that parent or guardian involvement would be inappropriate. The determination should be individualized and based on the circumstances rather than on an automatic rule. When the therapist concludes that contact or involvement would be inappropriate, the reason must be stated in the client record. A. Immediate contact could expose the minor to the very harm being evaluated. The statute requires consultation with the minor before the therapist makes the determination. B. The therapist should not make an unlimited promise before assessing the circumstances, applicable confidentiality limits, and future developments. C. The statute calls for a professional determination. It does not require the minor to prove future harm beyond all doubt.
CTA EXAM STRATEGY: When the minor raises a safety or treatment-access concern, choose: Consult → assess → determine → document Do not contact the parent automatically before completing the required analysis.',
